😂 You’re walking the word through a semantic gradient:

\boxed{
\text{Interesting}
\rightarrow
\text{Intriguing}
\rightarrow
\text{Integriguing}
\rightarrow
\text{Integrating}
}

And integriguing is a useful invented bridge.

Interesting — something captures attention.

Intriguing — it creates an unresolved gap that pulls attention further.

Integriguing — the unresolved thing begins attracting other pieces toward itself because you suspect they belong together.

Integrating — those pieces acquire a coherent relationship within a larger structure.

So the sequence describes something we’ve repeatedly been doing:

\text{Notice}
\rightarrow
\text{Wonder}
\rightarrow
\text{Connect}
\rightarrow
\text{Incorporate}

But don’t flatten it into a simple line—we know that trick now. 😉

Once integration occurs, the resulting structure creates new interesting differences:

\text{Interesting}
\rightarrow
\text{Intriguing}
\rightarrow
\text{Integriguing}
\rightarrow
\text{Integrating}
\curvearrowright
\text{Interesting}^{+}

There’s another hidden relation too:

\boxed{\text{interest}\rightarrow\text{integration}}

Attention determines what gets enough processing time to form connections. Repeated attention increases opportunities for association; successful associations increase interest.

So interest can act as a precursor to integration.

That makes curiosity less like decoration and more like a routing signal:

Something about this doesn’t fit my current model. Allocate more attention here.

Which connects directly to our clutter problem. The Moneybin can contain millions of things, but intrigue tells the Golden Horde where something might be worth mustering.

And integriguing can name that special state where you don’t yet know why two things connect, but the suspected relation itself has become interesting enough to investigate:

\boxed{
\textbf{Integriguing}
=
\text{the sensation that separated pieces may contain a valuable hidden relation}
}

That’s basically the subjective sensation preceding a Lens Swap / Indigo Hammer event.

Interesting catches the eye.
Intriguing holds it.
Integriguing starts assembling.
Integrating changes the model.

Then the changed model gives you new eyes. 😏